 title="Trip.com Group">Trip.com Group</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label">Website</th><td class="infobox-data"><span class="url"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.skyscanner.net/" class="external text">skyscanner.net</a></span></td></tr></tbody></table>

<p id="mwEg"><b id="mwEw">Skyscanner Ltd.</b> is a British <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Search_aggregator" title="Search aggregator" id="mwFA">search aggregator</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Travel_agency" title="Travel agency" id="mwFQ">travel agency</a> based in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edinburgh" title="Edinburgh" id="mwFg">Edinburgh</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotland" title="Scotland" id="mwFw">Scotland</a> and operating since 2002.</p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwGA" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwGQ" aria-labelledby="History"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="History">History</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Skyscanner&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: History"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwGg">The company was formed by three <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_technology" title="Information technology" id="mwGw">information technology</a> professionals.<sup about="#mwt21" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-3" id="mwHA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwHQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHw">]</span></span></a></sup> The Edinburgh office was opened in 2004.<sup about="#mwt24" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-4"}}'><a href="#cite_note-4" id="mwIA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwIQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwIg">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwIw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwJA">In 2007, Scottish Equity Partners (SEP) invested <span style="white-space: nowrap" about="#mwt25" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwJQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"GBP","href":"./Template:GBP"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2.5"}},"i":0}}]}'>£2.5</span> million in the company for a 40% stake.<sup about="#mwt28" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-5" id="mwJg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwJw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKA">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKQ">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2009, Skyscanner reported its first profit.<sup about="#mwt31" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-6"}}'><a href="#cite_note-6" id="mwKg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwKw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLA">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwLg">In 2011, Skyscanner acquired <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoombu" title="Zoombu" id="mwLw">Zoombu</a>.<sup about="#mwt34" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-7" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-7" id="mwMA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMg">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMw">]</span></span></a></sup> Skyscanner opened an office in Singapore in September 2011, which is the headquarters for its Asia-Pacific operations.<sup about="#mwt37" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-8"}}'><a href="#cite_note-8" id="mwNA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwNQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNg">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNw">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2012, a Beijing office was added, as Skyscanner began a partnership with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baidu" title="Baidu" id="mwOA">Baidu</a>, China's largest search engine.<sup about="#mwt40" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-9" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-9"}}'><a href="#cite_note-9" id="mwOQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwOg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwOw">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwPA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwPQ">By 2013, the company employed over 180 people.<sup about="#mwt43" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-america_10-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"america"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-america-10"}}'><a href="#cite_note-america-10" id="mwPg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwPw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQA">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQQ">]</span></span></a></sup> In February 2013, Skyscanner announced plans to open a United States base in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miami" title="Miami" id="mwQg">Miami</a>.<sup about="#mwt44" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-america_10-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"america"}}'><a href="#cite_note-america-10" id="mwQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwRA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRQ">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRg">]</span></span></a></sup> In October 2013, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sequoia_Capital" title="Sequoia Capital" id="mwRw">Sequoia Capital</a> purchased an interest in Skyscanner that valued the company at $800 million.<sup about="#mwt47" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-11" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-11"}}'><a href="#cite_note-11" id="mwSA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwSQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwSg">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwSw">]</span></span></a></sup> In June 2014, Skyscanner acquired Youbibi, a travel search engine company based in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shenzhen" title="Shenzhen" id="mwTA">Shenzhen</a>, China.<sup about="#mwt50" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-12" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-12"}}'><a href="#cite_note-12" id="mwTQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwTg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwTw">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwUQ">By February 2015, the company employed 600 people, double the employment of 18 months earlier.<sup about="#mwt53" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-13" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-13"}}'><a href="#cite_note-13" id="mwUg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwUw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVA">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwVg">In January 2016, the company raised $192 million based on a $1.6 billion valuation for the company.<sup about="#mwt56" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-14" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-14"}}'><a href="#cite_note-14" id="mwVw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwWA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWQ">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWg">]</span></span></a></sup> SEP, which owned a 40% stake in 2007, sold 5% through the exchange.<sup about="#mwt59" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-15" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-15"}}'><a href="#cite_note-15" id="mwWw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwXA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwXQ">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwXg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwXw">In November 2016, a Chinese company <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trip.com_Group" title="Trip.com Group" id="mwYA">Trip.com Group</a> (formerly Ctrip) bought Skyscanner for $1.75 billion.<sup about="#mwt62" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-16" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-16"}}'><a href="#cite_note-16" id="mwYQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwYg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwYw">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZA">]</span></span></a></sup> Following the sale to Ctrip, Skyscanner's largest shareholder, SEP, completed its exit from the business.<sup about="#mwt65" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-17" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-17"}}'><a href="#cite_note-17" id="mwZQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwZg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZw">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwaA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwaQ">In 2017, Ctrip bought the Trip.com domain and launched <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trip.com" title="Trip.com" id="mwag">Trip.com</a>. The original platform became a subsidiary of Skyscanner.<sup about="#mwt68" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-18" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-18"}}'><a href="#cite_note-18" id="mwaw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwbA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbQ">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwbw">In 2020, after the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic" title="COVID-19 pandemic" id="mwcA">COVID-19 pandemic</a>, the company announced that it would lay off 300 employees (20% of its staff). It was likely to close two offices in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budapest,_Hungary" title="Budapest, Hungary" class="mw-redirect" id="mwcQ">Budapest, Hungary</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sofia,_Bulgaria" title="Sofia, Bulgaria" class="mw-redirect" id="mwcg">Sofia, Bulgaria</a>.<sup about="#mwt71" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-19" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-19"}}'><a href="#cite_note-19" id="mwcw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwdA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdQ">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwdw">In May 2025, the company announced the appointment of Bryan Batista, its chief operations officer, as its new chief executive replacing John Mangelaars.<sup about="#mwt74" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:0_20-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":0"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:0-20"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:0-20" id="mweA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mweQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mweg">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwew">]</span></span></a></sup> Batista took up the role from 1 June 2025.<sup about="#mwt75" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:0_20-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":0"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:0-20" id="mwfA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwfQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfg">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwgA" aria-labelledby="Product"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Product">Product</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Skyscanner&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section: Product"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwgQ">Skyscanner is often listed among websites that help find <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheap_flights" title="Cheap flights" class="mw-redirect" id="mwgg">cheap flights</a>.<sup about="#mwt78" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-21" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-21"}}'><a href="#cite_note-21" id="mwgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwhA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhQ">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhg">]</span></span></a></sup> Its "Everywhere" feature allows users to find cheapest global destinations from a city.<sup about="#mwt81" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-22" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-22"}}'><a href="#cite_note-22" id="mwhw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwiA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwiQ">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwig">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

